narrative:
## Working with Sets

Sets shine when you need to answer membership questions and filter data based on presence or absence.

### The Three Core Operations

```xiom
let users: Set[Str] = Set[Str].new();
users.insert("alice");
users.insert("bob");

users.contains("alice");
users.remove("alice");
```

`.insert()` adds, `.contains()` checks, `.remove()` deletes.

### Duplicate Detection

```xiom
fn has_duplicates(items: Vec[Str]) -> Bool {
  let seen: Set[Str] = Set[Str].new();
  for item in items {
    if seen.contains(item) { return true; }
    seen.insert(item);
  }
  return false;
}
```

This function uses a Set to detect if any item appears more than once — the Set remembers what we've seen.

### Filtering a List

```xiom
fn filter_by_allowed(names: Vec[Str], allowed: Set[Str]) -> Vec[Str] {
  let result: Vec[Str] = Vec[Str].new();
  for name in names {
    if allowed.contains(name) {
      result.push(name);
    }
  }
  return result;
}
```

### Full Program: Festival Matcher

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let my_artists: Set[Str] = Set[Str].new();
  my_artists.insert("Luna");
  my_artists.insert("Neon Waves");
  my_artists.insert("The Circuits");

  let lineup: Set[Str] = Set[Str].new();
  lineup.insert("Neon Waves");
  lineup.insert("DJ Spark");

  if my_artists.contains("Neon Waves") && lineup.contains("Neon Waves") {
    io.println("Neon Waves is playing!");
  }

  if my_artists.contains("Luna") && !lineup.contains("Luna") {
    io.println("Luna is not playing");
  }

  return 0;
}
```

